Testing MathML transcription with Russian mathematical Braille.

The expected output uses Unicode Braille patterns. Structural MathML is
linearized with the same PUA contract as Liblouis' ru-math.uti. The signs are
based on GOST R 58511-2019 and the 2010 Russian mathematical Braille manual in
Russian_braille_info.

Integer fractions, powers, indices and root indices use the shortened forms
required by GOST, selected locally by liblouisutdml without MathCAT. General
symbolic structures remain covered separately. The suite also covers corrected
operators and arrows from Liblouis 3.38.0, promille,
digit grouping, mathematical line breaks, recognized brackets, left and
multiple indices, GOST italic/bold signs and Russian text/formula/punctuation
boundaries.

The runtime consists only of liblouisutdml and Liblouis 3.38.0. MathCAT was an
independent reference during development and is not loaded or called.

The 2010 extensions are selected directly from ordinary presentation MathML:
periodic and mixed numbers, compact or delimited fractions, named functions,
scripts, marks, vectors, geometry, sets and logic.  Layouts which cannot be
inferred safely from an ordinary `mtable` use an explicit formatter contract:

* `data-braille-layout="column-arithmetic"` or the intent token
  `:column-arithmetic` suppresses the number sign after the first row;
* `data-braille-layout="long-division"` or `:long-division` applies the same
  continuation-number rule to long division;
* standard MathML `mstack` and `mlongdiv` produce real aligned rows; the
  legacy `data-braille-layout` values remain covered for older producers;
* `data-braille-frame="joining-left"` and `joining-right` select the frame
  side, keep the mandatory blank, and produce one real line per table row;
  the older `joining` and `:joining-frame` spellings mean a left frame.

These attributes only convey print layout that MathML itself does not encode;
they do not invoke or depend on MathCAT.
